  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(t)=-2x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[2,\\text{\u00a0}2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{\u00a0}0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$",
